  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":1,"t":"single_choice","p":"A hospital records each patient''s blood group. Which classification is correct?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A hospital records each patient''s blood group. Which classification is correct?"}]}]},"e":"Blood group is a label, not a number: it is qualitative (categorical) data. It cannot be ordered or averaged, which is why a bar chart is appropriate and a mean is not.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"classification of data","st":"qualitative versus quantitative","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":1,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Ask whether the values are labels or amounts. If you cannot sensibly average them, they are qualitative.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["Qualitative (categorical), and a bar chart is appropriate","Quantitative continuous, because blood is measured","Quantitative discrete, because there are four groups","Qualitative, and the mean blood group should be calculated"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":2,"t":"single_choice","p":"The salaries in a small company are $22 000, $24 000, $25 000, $26 000 and $180 000. Which statement best describes the effect of the $180 000 salary?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The salaries in a small company are "},{"type":"text","text":"$22 000, $"},{"type":"text","text":"24 000, "},{"type":"text","text":"$25 000, $"},{"type":"text","text":"26 000 and "},{"type":"text","text":"$180 000. Which statement best describes the effect of the $"},{"type":"text","text":"180 000 salary?"}]}]},"e":"The mean is 277 000 ÷ 5 = $55 400 while four of the five salaries are below $26 000, so the mean is pulled up by the outlier. The median ($25 000) is unaffected and describes a typical salary better.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"mean, mode and median","st":"outliers and choice of measure","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":2,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"An outlier moves the mean because the mean uses every value''s size; the median only uses position.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["It inflates the mean, so the median describes a typical salary better","It inflates the median more than the mean","It has no effect because it is a single value","It makes the mode the best measure of centre"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":3,"t":"single_choice","p":"A grouped frequency table has classes 0 ≤ x < 10, 10 ≤ x < 20, 20 ≤ x < 30 with frequencies 5, 9 and 6. Which midpoint must be used for the class 10 ≤ x < 20 when estimating the mean?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A grouped frequency table has classes 0 ≤ x < 10, 10 ≤ x < 20, 20 ≤ x < 30 with frequencies 5, 9 and 6. Which midpoint must be used for the class 10 ≤ x < 20 when estimating the mean?"}]}]},"e":"The midpoint of a class is the average of its boundaries: (10 + 20)/2 = 15. Using 10 or 20 would place every value in that class at a boundary, which systematically skews the estimate.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"grouped data","st":"class midpoints","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":3,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Midpoint = (lower boundary + upper boundary) ÷ 2, not one of the boundaries.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["15","10, the lower boundary","20, the upper boundary","9, the frequency"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":4,"t":"single_choice","p":"A histogram has one class of width 5 with frequency 20 and an adjacent class of width 10 with frequency 20. The bar for the narrower class is drawn taller. By what factor?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A histogram has one class of width 5 with frequency 20 and an adjacent class of width 10 with frequency 20. The bar for the narrower class is drawn taller. By what factor?"}]}]},"e":"Frequency density is frequency ÷ width: 20/5 = 4 for the narrow class and 20/10 = 2 for the wide one, so the narrow bar is twice as tall. Equal frequencies do NOT give equal bar heights when widths differ.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"histograms","st":"frequency density with unequal widths","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":4,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"In a histogram, area — not height — represents frequency. Height = frequency ÷ class width.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["2","1 — equal frequencies must give equal bars","0.5","4"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":5,"t":"single_choice","p":"A cumulative frequency curve shows 40 students in total. Reading across at cumulative frequency 10 gives the value 23. What have you found?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A cumulative frequency curve shows 40 students in total. Reading across at cumulative frequency 10 gives the value 23. What have you found?"}]}]},"e":"The lower quartile is the value below which a quarter of the data lies: 40 ÷ 4 = 10, so the x-value at cumulative frequency 10 is Q1 = 23. The median would be read at 20 and Q3 at 30.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"cumulative frequency","st":"reading quartiles from a curve","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":5,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"On a cumulative frequency curve, read n/4, n/2 and 3n/4 on the vertical axis to find Q1, the median and Q3.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["The lower quartile, Q1 = 23","The median, 23","The upper quartile, Q3 = 23","The interquartile range, 23"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":6,"t":"single_choice","p":"Two classes sit the same test. Class A has mean 62 and standard deviation 4; Class B has mean 62 and standard deviation 11. Which statement is correct?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two classes sit the same test. Class A has mean 62 and standard deviation 4; Class B has mean 62 and standard deviation 11. Which statement is correct?"}]}]},"e":"A larger standard deviation means the values are more spread out around the same mean. Both classes average 62, but Class B''s marks are far more variable, so individual performances are less predictable.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"standard deviation","st":"interpreting standard deviation","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":6,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Standard deviation measures spread, not level. Equal means say nothing about consistency.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["Class B has more variable marks, so its results are less consistent","Class B has a higher average mark","The standard deviations show Class A has more outliers","The two classes performed identically because the means are equal"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":7,"t":"single_choice","p":"A stem-and-leaf diagram has rows 3 | 2 4 6 and 4 | 1 1 1 5 with the key ''4 | 1 means 41''. What is the mode?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A stem-and-leaf diagram has rows 3 | 2 4 6 and 4 | 1 1 1 5 with the key ''4 | 1 means 41''. What is the mode?"}]}]},"e":"The leaf 1 appears three times on stem 4, giving the value 41 three times — more often than any other value. Reading the key correctly is essential: on stem 4 the leaf 1 is 41, not 1.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"stem-and-leaf diagrams","st":"reading a stem-and-leaf key","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":7,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Always read the key first. The stem supplies the tens and the leaf the units in this example.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["41","1","46 (the highest value on the first row)","There is no mode"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":8,"t":"single_choice","p":"A box plot has minimum 12, Q1 18, median 25, Q3 31 and maximum 60. Which statement best explains the shape of the distribution?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A box plot has minimum 12, Q1 18, median 25, Q3 31 and maximum 60. Which statement best explains the shape of the distribution?"}]}]},"e":"The upper whisker runs from 31 to 60, which is much longer than the lower whisker (12 to 18), and the median is closer to Q1. That indicates positive skew: a tail of high values.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"five-number summary and box plots","st":"shape from a box plot","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":8,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Compare whisker lengths and the position of the median inside the box; that is the evidence for skew.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["It is positively skewed — the upper whisker is much longer","It is symmetrical because the median lies inside the box","It is negatively skewed because the minimum is small","The shape cannot be judged from a box plot"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":9,"t":"single_choice","p":"Eight students scored 3, 5, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9 on a quiz. A ninth student scores 10. How does the median change?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Eight students scored 3, 5, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9 on a quiz. A ninth student scores 10. How does the median change?"}]}]},"e":"With eight values the median is (6 + 7)/2 = 6.5. Adding a ninth value gives 3, 5, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 9, 10, whose median is the 5th value = 7. The mean rises by less than a mark, but the median moves to an actual data value.","m":3,"df":"medium","tp":"mean, mode and median","st":"effect of n on the median","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":9,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"For an even number of values the median is the mean of the two middle values; with an odd number it is a single value.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["It rises from 6.5 to 7","It stays at 6.5","It rises from 6.5 to 7.5","It falls because more values are added"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":10,"t":"single_choice","p":"A cumulative frequency table ends with a total frequency of 50. A candidate reads the median at cumulative frequency 50. What error have they made?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A cumulative frequency table ends with a total frequency of 50. A candidate reads the median at cumulative frequency 50. What error have they made?"}]}]},"e":"The median is read at n/2 = 25, not at the total. Cumulating to 50 locates the maximum, so the candidate has found the largest value rather than the middle one.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"cumulative frequency","st":"misreading a cumulative axis","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":10,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"The vertical axis of a cumulative frequency diagram runs to n, so quartiles are read at n/4, n/2 and 3n/4.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["They read the maximum instead of the median; the median is at 25","They should have read at 50 ÷ 4","Nothing — the median is always at the total","They should have subtracted 50 from the total"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":11,"t":"single_choice","p":"For the grouped table 0 ≤ x < 10, 10 ≤ x < 20, 20 ≤ x < 30 with frequencies 4, 7, 5, what is the estimated mean?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For the grouped table 0 ≤ x < 10, 10 ≤ x < 20, 20 ≤ x < 30 with frequencies 4, 7, 5, what is the estimated mean?"}]}]},"e":"Σf x = 4(5) + 7(15) + 5(25) = 20 + 105 + 125 = 250 and Σf = 16, so the estimated mean is 250 ÷ 16 = 15.625.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"grouped data","st":"estimated mean from midpoints","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":11,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Estimated mean = Σ(f × midpoint) ÷ Σf. Write both sums before dividing.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["15.625","15 (the middle class midpoint)","16 (Σf ÷ 1, a misread)","20 (the highest midpoint)"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":12,"t":"single_choice","p":"A histogram bar covers 20 ≤ x < 30 with height (frequency density) 1.4. What is the frequency of that class?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A histogram bar covers 20 ≤ x < 30 with height (frequency density) 1.4. What is the frequency of that class?"}]}]},"e":"Frequency = density × class width = 1.4 × 10 = 14. The area of the bar is the frequency, which is why density must be multiplied by the width.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"histograms","st":"frequency from density","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":12,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"frequency = frequency density × class width; equivalently, the area of each bar.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["14","1.4","28","140"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":13,"t":"single_choice","p":"A data set is 4, 4, 4, 4, 4. What is its standard deviation, and what does that tell you?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A data set is 4, 4, 4, 4, 4. What is its standard deviation, and what does that tell you?"}]}]},"e":"Every value equals the mean 4, so each deviation is 0, Σ(x − x̄)² = 0 and σ = 0. A standard deviation of zero means there is no spread at all.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"standard deviation","st":"standard deviation of a constant set","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":13,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Standard deviation is the root mean square deviation; if there are no deviations, it is zero.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["0 — there is no spread","4 — the mean is 4","2 — half the mean","It cannot be calculated from five values"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":14,"t":"single_choice","p":"Two runners'' times (minutes) over 6 races: A: 30, 32, 33, 34, 36, 37. B: 28, 31, 33, 33, 35, 44. Which comparison is supported by the data?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two runners'' times (minutes) over 6 races: A: 30, 32, 33, 34, 36, 37. B: 28, 31, 33, 33, 35, 44. Which comparison is supported by the data?"}]}]},"e":"Both have the same mean (202 ÷ 6 ≈ 33.67) and the same median (33), but B has a much larger spread (range 16 against 7, and larger deviations), so B is less consistent even though the averages match.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"comparing data sets","st":"comparing centre and spread","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":14,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Compare a measure of centre AND a measure of spread; two distributions can share a centre and differ in consistency.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["The medians are equal but B is less consistent","B has the higher median","A has the larger range","A is less consistent because its values are closer together"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":15,"t":"single_choice","p":"A line graph of a shop''s monthly sales rises steeply from January to March, then is flat from March to August. Which conclusion is justified?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A line graph of a shop''s monthly sales rises steeply from January to March, then is flat from March to August. Which conclusion is justified?"}]}]},"e":"The graph supports a statement about the trend in the recorded months only: sales grew early in the year and then held steady. It does not explain why, and it says nothing about September onwards.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"line graphs","st":"trend versus cause","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":15,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"A graph shows what happened in the data, not why. Keep causal claims out of a data interpretation.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["Sales increased early in the year and then stayed roughly constant","The shop will close because sales stopped growing","The shop''s advertising caused the March increase","Sales were highest in August"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":16,"t":"single_choice","p":"A market researcher records four variables. Which one of them is continuous data?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A market researcher records four variables. Which one of them is continuous data?"}]}]},"e":"The mass of a parcel can take any value in an interval, so it is continuous. Shoe size, the number of siblings and a house number are counts or labels.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"classification of data","st":"discrete versus continuous","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":16,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"If the value can, in principle, be measured more and more precisely, it is continuous; if you count it, it is discrete.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["The mass of a parcel, measured to the nearest gram","A shoe size","The number of siblings a student has","A house number"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":17,"t":"single_choice","p":"For the data 3, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12, 14, 15, 18 (using the median-excluded quartile method), what is the interquartile range?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For the data 3, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12, 14, 15, 18 (using the median-excluded quartile method), what is the interquartile range?"}]}]},"e":"There are 9 values, so the median is the 5th value = 10. The lower half is 3, 5, 7, 8 with Q1 = 6, and the upper half is 12, 14, 15, 18 with Q3 = 14.5. IQR = 14.5 − 6 = 8.5.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"five-number summary and box plots","st":"quartiles with an odd n","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":17,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Split the ordered data at the median and exclude the median itself when n is odd, then average the middle two of each half.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["8.5","10","15 (maximum − minimum)","5 (Q3 − median)"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":18,"t":"single_choice","p":"A grouped table has classes with widths 10, 10, 20 and 20 and frequencies 6, 9, 10 and 6. Which statement about drawing a histogram is correct?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A grouped table has classes with widths 10, 10, 20 and 20 and frequencies 6, 9, 10 and 6. Which statement about drawing a histogram is correct?"}]}]},"e":"Because the widths differ, the bar heights must be frequency densities: 0.6, 0.9, 0.5 and 0.3. Using the raw frequencies would make the 20-wide classes look twice as important as they are.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"grouped data","st":"density for mixed widths","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":18,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Divide each frequency by its own class width before drawing, so that all areas remain comparable.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["The four densities are 0.6, 0.9, 0.5 and 0.3","The bars should be 6, 9, 10 and 6 high","All four bars are the same height because the total is 31","The widths cannot be compared on a histogram"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":19,"t":"single_choice","p":"A data set has mean 20, median 22 and mode 30. Which statement is most consistent with these values?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A data set has mean 20, median 22 and mode 30. Which statement is most consistent with these values?"}]}]},"e":"Mean < median < mode is the classic signature of a negatively (left) skewed distribution, where a few small values pull the mean down while the mode sits at the most frequent higher value.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"mean, mode and median","st":"mean, median and mode together","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":19,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Compare the order of mean, median and mode: a mean below the median suggests a low-value tail.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["The data are negatively skewed, with a tail of small values","The data are perfectly symmetrical","The data are positively skewed, with a tail of large values","The three measures cannot disagree"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":20,"t":"single_choice","p":"A survey asks students to choose one of five favourite sports. Which is the most suitable representation?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A survey asks students to choose one of five favourite sports. Which is the most suitable representation?"}]}]},"e":"The data are categorical with five named groups, so a bar chart (or a pie chart) compares the counts. A histogram and a cumulative frequency curve require numerical classes.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"classification of data","st":"choosing a representation","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":20,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Histograms, cumulative curves and box plots need numerical data. Named categories use bar or pie charts.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["A bar chart, because the data are categorical","A histogram, because there are five groups","A cumulative frequency curve, because the counts add to the total","A box plot, because it shows the spread of sports"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":21,"t":"single_choice","p":"A line graph of a child''s height is plotted monthly for two years and shows a steep rise. Why is a line graph appropriate here but misleading if the axes do not start at zero?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A line graph of a child''s height is plotted monthly for two years and shows a steep rise. Why is a line graph appropriate here but misleading if the axes do not start at zero?"}]}]},"e":"A line graph shows a continuous change over time, which suits monthly height. If the vertical axis starts at, say, 140 cm instead of 0, small changes look dramatic, which exaggerates the trend.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"line graphs","st":"misleading axes","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":21,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"Check both axes before interpreting a line graph; a truncated vertical scale magnifies every fluctuation.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["It suits change over time, but a truncated vertical axis exaggerates small changes","It is inappropriate because height is continuous data","It is appropriate because height is a category","A pie chart would show the change better"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":22,"t":"single_choice","p":"A data set of 5 values has Σ(x − x̄)² = 80. What is the population standard deviation?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A data set of 5 values has Σ(x − x̄)² = 80. What is the population standard deviation?"}]}]},"e":"Variance = 80 ÷ 5 = 16, so σ = √16 = 4. Dividing by 5 (the number of values) is what distinguishes the population standard deviation from the sample version.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"standard deviation","st":"computing sigma from the sum of squares","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":22,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"","next_time_hint":"σ = √(Σ(x − x̄)² / n). Divide by n first, then take the square root — not the other way round.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","option_labels":["4","16","8","80"],"option_key":[1],"hints":[]},"marking":""},{"n":23,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A hospital records, for each patient admitted in a week: age (years), blood group, number of previous admissions, and body temperature (°C).\n\n(a) Classify each of the four variables as qualitative or quantitative.\n(b) For the quantitative variables, state whether each is discrete or continuous.\n(c) Justify your classification of ''number of previous admissions''.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A hospital records, for each patient admitted in a week: age (years), blood group, number of previous admissions, and body temperature (°C)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Classify each of the four variables as qualitative or quantitative.\n(b) For the quantitative variables, state whether each is discrete or continuous.\n(c) Justify your classification of ''number of previous admissions''."}]}]},"e":"Blood group is qualitative; age and temperature are quantitative continuous; the number of previous admissions is quantitative discrete because it is a count with gaps between permitted values.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"classification of data","st":"classification of data","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":23,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","strand":"B(i)","next_time_hint":"Classify in two steps: is it a label or an amount, and if an amount, is it counted or measured?","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Name the qualitative variable.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["A qualitative variable is a label or category, not an amount.","Which of the four cannot sensibly be averaged?"],"strand":"B(i)","accepted":["blood group"]},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State which quantitative variable is discrete.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["A discrete variable is counted in whole steps.","Age and temperature can be measured more precisely; admissions cannot."],"strand":"B(ii)","accepted":["number of previous admissions"],"keywords":[["number"],["previous"],["admissions"]],"minKeywordGroups":3},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Justify why ''number of previous admissions'' must be discrete.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Can a patient have 2.4 previous admissions?","The values jump from one whole number to the next."],"strand":"B(iii)","accepted":["you can only count whole admissions, so its values are separate whole numbers"],"keywords":[["count"],["whole"],["admissions"],["values"],["separate"],["numbers"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for blood group. (b) 2 marks: 1 for admissions discrete, 1 for age/temperature continuous. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''counted'', 1 for the consequence (no fractional values).","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for blood group. (b) 2 marks: 1 for admissions discrete, 1 for age/temperature continuous. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''counted'', 1 for the consequence (no fractional values).","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"In (c) explain the counting argument, not only the definition of discrete."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for blood group. (b) 2 marks: 1 for admissions discrete, 1 for age/temperature continuous. (c) 2 marks: 1 for ''counted'', 1 for the consequence (no fractional values)."},{"n":24,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The masses of 40 parcels, in kilograms, are summarised below.\n\n10 ≤ m < 15: 6 parcels\n15 ≤ m < 20: 14 parcels\n20 ≤ m < 30: 12 parcels\n30 ≤ m < 40: 8 parcels\n\n(a) State the class width of the third class.\n(b) Calculate the estimated mean mass, using midpoints.\n(c) Explain why the class 20 ≤ m < 30 has a lower frequency density than 15 ≤ m < 20 even though it has almost the same frequency.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The masses of 40 parcels, in kilograms, are summarised below."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10 ≤ m < 15: 6 parcels\n15 ≤ m < 20: 14 parcels\n20 ≤ m < 30: 12 parcels\n30 ≤ m < 40: 8 parcels"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the class width of the third class.\n(b) Calculate the estimated mean mass, using midpoints.\n(c) Explain why the class 20 ≤ m < 30 has a lower frequency density than 15 ≤ m < 20 even though it has almost the same frequency."}]}]},"e":"Σf x = 6(12.5) + 14(17.5) + 12(25) + 8(35) = 75 + 245 + 300 + 280 = 900, so the estimated mean is 900/40 = 22.5 kg. Densities are 1.2, 2.8, 1.2 and 0.8; the third class is less dense than the second because its frequency is spread over twice the width.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"grouped data","st":"grouped data","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":24,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Estimated mean = Σ(f × midpoint) ÷ Σf. Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the class width of 20 ≤ m < 30.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Width = upper boundary − lower boundary.","30 − 20."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":10,"tolerance":0,"unit":"kg"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate Σ(f × midpoint).","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Use the midpoint of each class: 12.5, 17.5, 25, 35.","Multiply each midpoint by its frequency and add the four products."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":900,"tolerance":0,"unit":"kg"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the estimated mean mass, in kilograms.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Divide your Σ(f × midpoint) by the total frequency.","The frequencies add to 40."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":22.5,"tolerance":0.01,"unit":"kg"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain the lower frequency density of the third class.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width.","Compare 12/10 with 14/5."],"strand":"C(iv)","accepted":["its class width is twice as large, so the same frequency is spread over a wider interval, giving a lower density: 1.2 against 2.8"],"keywords":[["class"],["width"],["twice"],["large"],["frequency"],["spread"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 10. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the four products, 1 for 900. (c) 2 marks: 1 for dividing by 40, 1 for 22.5. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the density formula, 1 for the comparison 1.2 vs 2.8.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark for 10. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the four products, 1 for 900. (c) 2 marks: 1 for dividing by 40, 1 for 22.5. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the density formula, 1 for the comparison 1.2 vs 2.8.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","statistics"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Build the grouped table in the statistics workspace as a check, but record your answers in the boxes.","statistics_chart":"histogram"},"marking":"(a) 1 mark for 10. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the four products, 1 for 900. (c) 2 marks: 1 for dividing by 40, 1 for 22.5. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the density formula, 1 for the comparison 1.2 vs 2.8."},{"n":25,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The waiting times, in minutes, of 15 patients are:\n\n4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12, 13, 15, 16, 18, 20, 23, 25, 28\n\n(a) State the median.\n(b) Calculate the lower and upper quartiles.\n(c) Calculate the interquartile range.\n(d) A waiting time of 45 minutes is recorded the next day. Explain, using the 1.5 × IQR rule, whether it is an outlier.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The waiting times, in minutes, of 15 patients are:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12, 13, 15, 16, 18, 20, 23, 25, 28"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the median.\n(b) Calculate the lower and upper quartiles.\n(c) Calculate the interquartile range.\n(d) A waiting time of 45 minutes is recorded the next day. Explain, using the 1.5 × IQR rule, whether it is an outlier."}]}]},"e":"Median 13; Q1 = 8; Q3 = 20; IQR = 12. The upper fence is 20 + 1.5(12) = 38, so 45 minutes lies beyond it and is an outlier. The box plot''s long upper whisker anticipates this.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"five-number summary and box plots","st":"five-number summary and box plots","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":25,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Q1 and Q3 are the medians of the lower and upper halves. The 1.5 × IQR fences identify outliers.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the median waiting time, in minutes.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["With 15 values, which position is the middle?","(15 + 1) ÷ 2 = 8th value."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":13,"tolerance":0,"unit":"minutes"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the lower quartile Q1, in minutes.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Exclude the median and take the lower seven values.","The middle of 4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12 is the 4th value."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":8,"tolerance":0,"unit":"minutes"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State the upper quartile Q3, in minutes.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The upper half is 15, 16, 18, 20, 23, 25, 28.","Its middle value is the 4th."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":20,"tolerance":0,"unit":"minutes"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate the IQR, in minutes.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The interquartile range is the upper quartile minus the lower quartile.","Subtract your Q1 from your Q3."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":12,"tolerance":0,"unit":"minutes"},{"id":"e","label":"(e)","prompt":"Decide whether 45 minutes is an outlier and justify with the fence value.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Upper fence = Q3 + 1.5 × IQR.","Compare 45 with 20 + 18."],"strand":"C(iv)","accepted":["yes, it is an outlier: the upper fence is 38 minutes and 45 exceeds it"],"keywords":[["outlier"],["upper"],["fence"],["minutes"],["exceeds"]],"minKeywordGroups":3}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for splitting at the median, 1 for 8. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the upper half, 1 for 20. (d) 1 mark for 12. (e) 2 marks: 1 for the fence 38, 1 for the correct conclusion.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for splitting at the median, 1 for 8. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the upper half, 1 for 20. (d) 1 mark for 12. (e) 2 marks: 1 for the fence 38, 1 for the correct conclusion.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"Enter the 15 waiting times in the statistics workspace and draw the box plot to check your quartiles.","statistics_chart":"box_plot"},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for splitting at the median, 1 for 8. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the upper half, 1 for 20. (d) 1 mark for 12. (e) 2 marks: 1 for the fence 38, 1 for the correct conclusion."},{"n":26,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A grouped frequency table for 50 journey times (minutes) is:\n\n0 ≤ t < 5: 4\n5 ≤ t < 10: 12\n10 ≤ t < 20: 18\n20 ≤ t < 30: 10\n30 ≤ t < 40: 6\n\n(a) Calculate the class width of each class.\n(b) Calculate the frequency density of the class 10 ≤ t < 20.\n(c) Explain why plain frequencies cannot be used as bar heights for this table.\n(d) Calculate the total area of the correct histogram and state what it represents.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A grouped frequency table for 50 journey times (minutes) is:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"0 ≤ t < 5: 4\n5 ≤ t < 10: 12\n10 ≤ t < 20: 18\n20 ≤ t < 30: 10\n30 ≤ t < 40: 6"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the class width of each class.\n(b) Calculate the frequency density of the class 10 ≤ t < 20.\n(c) Explain why plain frequencies cannot be used as bar heights for this table.\n(d) Calculate the total area of the correct histogram and state what it represents."}]}]},"e":"The densities 0.8, 2.4, 1.8, 1.0, 0.6 give areas 4, 12, 18, 10, 6 which total 50 — exactly the number of journeys. Area is frequency; height alone is not.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"histograms","st":"histograms","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp4-mock","question_number":26,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Draw a histogram only from frequency densities when class widths differ: area must equal frequency.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp4-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the class width of 20 ≤ t < 30.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["A class width is the upper boundary minus the lower boundary.","For 20 ≤ t < 30 use 30 − 20."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":10,"tolerance":0,"unit":"minutes"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the frequency density of 10 ≤ t < 20.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width.","18 ÷ 10."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":1.8,"tolerance":0.01},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain why frequencies cannot be used as bar heights here.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare the widths: 5 and 10 minutes.","A bar''s area must be proportional to the frequency."],"strand":"C(iii)","accepted":["the class widths are not equal, so equal frequencies would give different areas; heights must be density so that area represents frequency"],"keywords":[["class"],["widths"],["equal"],["frequencies"],["give"],["different"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate the total area of the histogram.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Add density × width for all five classes.","It must equal the total frequency."],"strand":"C(iv)","answer":50,"tolerance":0}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 1.8. (c) 2 marks: 1 for unequal widths, 1 for the area argument. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the summation, 1 for 50 = total frequency.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 1.8. (c) 2 marks: 1 for unequal widths, 1 for the area argument. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the summation, 1 for 50 = total frequency.","response_tools":["statistics","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"statistics","response_guidance":"Enter the grouped table in the statistics workspace: the chart will plot densities automatically when widths differ.","statistics_chart":"histogram"},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 1.8. (c) 2 marks: 1 for unequal widths, 1 for the area argument. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the summation, 1 for 50 = total frequency."}]'::jsonb) as x
